## Abstract (paraphrase from pdftotext)

The authors extend randomized benchmarking (RB) to gate sets that form (representations of) finite groups G, without requiring the group to be a unitary 2-design. They derive:
- A K-term exponential decay model for the RB survival probability, where K = number of inequivalent irreps of G in the relevant representation.
- Stability of the RB estimator under Haar-approximate sampling: replacing exact Haar sampling from G by a length-b random walk on a generator set perturbs the estimator by O(δ² · b · m), where δ is the total-variation distance to Haar.
- A concrete case study on the monomial-unitary group MU(d, n) — unitaries whose nonzero entries are n-th roots of unity — which contains T for n ≥ 8 and admits O(d) multiplication and inversion.

Numerical results (Section 7): Table 1 verifies fidelity extraction on MU(d, 8) at d ∈ {64, 128, 1024}; Tables 3-5 verify Clifford generator RB at n=5; Figure 1 illustrates three-protocol equivalence.

